.TH "UMAMI" "1" "2024" "umami 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
umami \[em] log anomaly detection and structural tokenization
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]umami\f[R] \f[I]SUBCOMMAND\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
[\f[I]ARGS\f[R]\&...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]umami\f[R] learns the \f[I]structure\f[R] of streaming log events
and surfaces only the new, changed, and anomalous lines, filtering out
repetitive, already\-seen traffic.
.PP
The detection pipeline has four stages:
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]Tokenize\f[R] \[em] split each line into typed tokens (literals,
delimiters, timestamps, IP addresses, quoted strings, numbers, JSON
keys).
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]Structure signature\f[R] \[em] hash the static tokens (literals and
keys), plus a placeholder for each dynamic value, with FNV\-1a into a
stable 64\-bit fingerprint.
Dynamic values are numbers, timestamps, IPs, and any word that contains
a digit or a \f[B]/\f[R] (hostnames like \f[I]web01\f[R], ids, paths,
URLs).
Lines that differ only in dynamic values share the same signature.
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]Profile\f[R] \[em] look up the (system, signature) pair in the
store.
Track event counts, first/last seen times, the values seen in each
parameter slot (up to 100 distinct values; past that the slot is treated
as free\-form and ignored), and inter\-arrival statistics.
.IP "4." 3
\f[B]Classify\f[R] \[em] compare the new event against the learned
profile and emit an anomaly kind, or suppress the event if it matches
normal behavior.
.PP
Anomaly kinds:
.TP
\f[B]never_before_seen\f[R]
The structure has not been seen on any system.
.TP
\f[B]new_in_system\f[R]
The structure has been seen on other systems but not this one.
.TP
\f[B]parameter_change\f[R]
The structure is known but a dynamic parameter has a new value.
.TP
\f[B]frequency_spike\f[R]
Events are arriving significantly faster than the learned baseline
(graded small, medium, or large by z\-score magnitude).
.TP
\f[B]event_stop\f[R]
A structure that arrives at a steady rate has been silent for more than
three of its mean intervals.
Reported once, until it is seen again.
.TP
\f[B]system_away\f[R]
A system has sent nothing for ten minutes.
Reported once, until it sends again.
.PP
\f[B]pipeline\f[R], \f[B]sim\f[R] and \f[B]ingest\f[R] run the detector
on \f[I]event time\f[R]: each line is observed at its own timestamp (ISO
8601, Apache CLF, or syslog; a syslog timestamp\[cq]s year is inferred),
and a line without one at the latest timestamp seen so far.
Silences are looked for once per minute of event time.
Replaying an old log therefore reports the stops and absences it
contains.
Input is processed line by line, so these commands work on live streams
(\f[B]tail \-f\f[R], \f[B]journalctl \-f\f[R]); invalid UTF\-8 is
replaced, not fatal.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.SS tokenize
\f[B]umami tokenize\f[R] [\f[B]\[en]tokenizer\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]]
[\f[B]\[en]pretty\f[R] \f[I]STYLE\f[R]] [\f[B]\[en]color\f[R]]
[\f[B]\[en]test\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R] [\f[B]\[en]fix\f[R]]
[\f[B]\[en]verbose\f[R]]] [\f[I]LINE\f[R]\&...]
.PP
Tokenize one or more log lines and print the result.
If no \f[I]LINE\f[R] arguments are given, lines are read from standard
input.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]tokenizer\f[R] \f[I]structure\f[R]|\f[I]legacy\f[R]
Select the tokenizer variant.
\f[B]structure\f[R] (default) \[em] the modern tokenizer that
understands \f[B]key=value\f[R] pairs (promoting the key to a
\f[B]JsonKey\f[R] token), quoted strings, and JSON key/value contexts.
\f[B]legacy\f[R] \[em] the original whitespace\-oriented tokenizer;
splits on whitespace, peels leading/trailing punctuation, and recognizes
IPv4 addresses.
Does not detect ISO 8601 timestamps.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]pretty\f[R] \f[I]test\f[R]|\f[I]colors\f[R]|\f[I]params\f[R]|\f[I]delimiters\f[R]|\f[I]static\f[R]
Output format (default: \f[B]test\f[R]).
\f[B]test\f[R] \[em] reference format: \f[B]T{text}\f[R] tokens joined
by spaces, where T is the numeric token type (0=literal, 1=delimiter,
2=timestamp, \&...).
\f[B]colors\f[R] \[em] original line text with ANSI color highlighting
per token type (use with \f[B]\[en]color\f[R] to activate).
\f[B]params\f[R] \[em] original line followed by a
\f[B]\[ha]\[ti]\[ti]\[ti]\f[R] marker row marking each dynamic
(parameter) token.
\f[B]delimiters\f[R] \[em] original line followed by a \f[B]\[ha]\f[R]
marker row marking each delimiter.
\f[B]static\f[R] \[em] the structure signature: structural tokens joined
by spaces, with \f[B]<*>\f[R] for each parameter.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]color\f[R]
Enable ANSI coloring (only meaningful with \f[B]\[en]pretty
colors\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\[en]test\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Run a golden test file instead of tokenizing input.
The file format is TSV: \f[I]input\f[R]xpected*, where expected is the
\f[B]test\f[R]\-format output.
Failures are printed to standard output.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]fix\f[R]
When used with \f[B]\[en]test\f[R], rewrite the file with the
tokenizer\[cq]s current output, updating any mismatched expected values
in\-place.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]verbose\f[R]
With \f[B]\[en]test\f[R], also print passing cases.
.SS Examples
.IP
.EX
# Tokenize a single line
umami tokenize \[dq]Jan 23 10:23:45 web01 sshd[1234]: ok\[dq]

# Show dynamic\-parameter positions
umami tokenize \-\-pretty params \[dq]level=info duration=3.14 status=ok\[dq]

# Colorized output
umami tokenize \-\-pretty colors \-\-color \[dq]disk full on /dev/sda1\[dq]

# Read from stdin; show only structural tokens
journalctl \-n 500 | umami tokenize \-\-pretty static

# Check a corpus file
umami tokenize \-\-test corpus/structure.tsv
.EE
.SS pipeline
\f[B]umami pipeline\f[R] [\f[I]DETECT OPTIONS\f[R]]
[\f[B]\[en]show\-all\f[R]]
.PP
Read log lines from standard input, run each through the detector, and
print anomalous lines to standard output.
Each output line is prefixed with the anomaly kind padded to 18
characters, then the original line.
\f[B]event_stop\f[R] and \f[B]system_away\f[R], which have no line,
print \f[B][\f[R]\f[I]system\f[R]\f[B]]\f[R] and the signature instead.
.PP
Without \f[B]\[en]show\-all\f[R], normal (non\-anomalous) lines are
suppressed.
.SS Detect options
These apply to \f[B]pipeline\f[R], \f[B]sim\f[R] and \f[B]ingest\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\[en]system\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
Attribute events to this system name (default: \f[B]stdin\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\[en]tagged\f[R]
Treat each input line as \f[I]system\f[R]ogline*, so several systems can
be multiplexed on one stream.
Lines without a tab are attributed to \f[B]\[en]system\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\[en]tokenizer\f[R] \f[I]structure\f[R]|\f[I]legacy\f[R]
Tokenizer variant (default: \f[B]structure\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\[en]parameters\f[R]
Also emit \f[B]parameter_change\f[R] anomalies when a known structure
receives a previously\-unseen parameter value.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]state\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Load learned profiles from \f[I]FILE\f[R] if it exists, and save them
back at the end of input and at most once a minute while input is idle,
so learning carries over between runs.
The file is replaced atomically.
A file saved with a different \f[B]\[en]tokenizer\f[R], or by an
incompatible version, is an error.
.SS Pipeline options
.TP
\f[B]\[en]show\-all\f[R]
Print every line; prefix normal lines with \f[B]ok\f[R] instead of
suppressing them.
.SS Examples
.IP
.EX
cat /var/log/syslog | umami pipeline \-\-system web01

# Show all lines annotated
cat syslog | umami pipeline \-\-show\-all

# Detect parameter changes too
cat access.log | umami pipeline \-\-parameters

# Follow the journal, remembering what was learned last time
journalctl \-f | umami pipeline \-\-state \[ti]/.cache/umami.json
.EE
.SS sim
\f[B]umami sim\f[R] [\f[I]DETECT OPTIONS\f[R]]
.PP
Consume all of standard input and print a summary of anomaly counts,
total event count, and the noise\-reduction percentage (fraction of
lines filtered as normal).
Takes the detect options described under \f[B]pipeline\f[R].
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
cat /var/log/syslog | umami sim \-\-system prod\-web01

events:    50000
anomalies: 222
reduction: 99.6%
profiles:  210
  event_stop         9
  frequency_spike    12
  never_before_seen  210
.EE
.SS ingest
\f[B]umami ingest\f[R] [\f[I]DETECT OPTIONS\f[R]]
.PP
Read log lines from standard input and emit one JSON object per anomaly
to standard output (newline\-delimited JSON).
Each JSON object has the fields: \f[B]system\f[R], \f[B]kind\f[R],
\f[B]score\f[R] (float 0\[en]1), \f[B]hash\f[R] (the 16\-hex\-digit
structure hash, or \f[B]null\f[R] for \f[B]system_away\f[R]),
\f[B]signature\f[R] (the structure, with \f[B]<*>\f[R] for each
parameter), \f[B]line\f[R] (original log line; empty for
\f[B]event_stop\f[R] and \f[B]system_away\f[R]).
Takes the detect options described under \f[B]pipeline\f[R].
.SS Examples
.IP
.EX
cat /var/log/syslog | umami ingest \-\-system web01 | jq .

# Multi\-system input
paste \-d\[aq]\[rs]t\[aq] systems.txt lines.txt | umami ingest \-\-tagged

# Example output line
{\[dq]system\[dq]:\[dq]web01\[dq],\[dq]kind\[dq]:\[dq]never_before_seen\[dq],\[dq]score\[dq]:1.00,
 \[dq]hash\[dq]:\[dq]96a870bdc586e53c\[dq],\[dq]signature\[dq]:\[dq]disk full on <*>\[dq],
 \[dq]line\[dq]:\[dq]disk full on /dev/sda1\[dq]}
.EE
.SS load
\f[B]umami load\f[R] [\f[B]\[en]count\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]]
[\f[B]\[en]tokenizer\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]] [\f[B]\[en]stdin\f[R]]
.PP
Throughput benchmark.
By default generates \f[I]N\f[R] synthetic log lines from four templates
and measures how fast the detector filters them.
With \f[B]\[en]stdin\f[R], reads from standard input instead.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]count\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Number of synthetic events to generate (default: \f[B]1000000\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\[en]tokenizer\f[R] \f[I]structure\f[R]|\f[I]legacy\f[R]
Tokenizer variant (default: \f[B]structure\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\[en]stdin\f[R]
Read lines from standard input instead of generating synthetic data.
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
umami load
umami load \-\-count 500000
cat big.log | umami load \-\-stdin
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
Success.
.TP
\f[B]1\f[R]
An error occurred (bad argument, I/O error, unreadable
\f[B]\[en]state\f[R] file, test failure).
A closed output pipe (\f[B]| head\f[R]) is not an error.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[B]corpus/structure.tsv\f[R], \f[B]corpus/legacy.tsv\f[R]
Golden test corpora in \f[I]input\f[R]xpected* TSV format, used by the
integration tests.
